INT. SERVICE ELEVATOR - GRAND HOTEL - DAY

A brass box, six feet square, smelling of lilies. In the center, a four-tier wedding cake on a rolling cart, wrapped in plastic like a fragile patient.

BERNADETTE QUARLES-IBARRA (50s, caterer's apron, clipboard) presses herself against the left wall. FERDINAND "FERDIE" ACHTERBERG-LOWE (60s, tuxedo, bow tie slightly crooked) holds the right. KWABENA OSEI-DRUMMOND (20s, bellhop pillbox hat) guards the buttons. LOTTIE FAIRCHILD-NAKAMURA (8, flower girl, wreath of daisies) sits cross-legged on the floor beside the cart, eating a rose off the top tier.

The elevator lurches. Everyone freezes. The cake sways. Bernadette's clipboard drops. Ferdie's hand shoots out to steady the cart. Kwabena stabs three buttons at once. Lottie licks icing off her wrist. The lift hums, hangs, hums again.

FERDIE
Is it stuck?

BERNADETTE
It is not stuck.

KWABENA
It's thinking.

LOTTIE
Can I have the other rose?

BERNADETTE
No.

FERDIE
Yes.

KWABENA
Sir, with respect, it's her cake.

FERDIE
It's my daughter's cake, and my granddaughter's rose.

BERNADETTE
That rose is sixty dollars.

LOTTIE
It tastes like soap.

The elevator drops six inches. The top tier slides. Four hands lunge. The cake settles. Nobody breathes. Ferdie wipes a smear of frosting from his lapel and holds out his hand, palm up, to Bernadette. She looks at it. Then she hands him a plastic fork.
